# Facile fabrication of nanoscale hierarchical porous zeolitic imidazolate frameworks for enhanced toluene adsorption capacity

Abstract The development of a simple and facile synthesis route is a highly desirable but challenging process in the fabrication of nanoscale hierarchical porous zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs). Herein we describe a facile method for rapidly synthesizing hierarchically porous ZIF90 nanocrystals (particle sizes of $\sim300\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) using hydroxyl double salts as intermediates at room temperature. The as-synthesized ZIF-90 contained hierarchical porous structures developed using a crystalline interior and a random stack of multiple nanoparticles. Both the morphology and particle size of ZIF-90 nanocrystals could be tuned by controlling the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ . Note that the as-synthesized hierarchically porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals exhibited higher thermal stability compared with the conventional ZIF-90. Because of the introduction of hierarchical porous structures, the resultant hierarchically porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals showed enhanced toluene adsorption capacity than those of conventional metal organic frameworks and zeolites.
Keywords Zeolitic imidazolate framework-90; Nanoscale; Green and rapid synthesis; Hierarchical porous structure; Enhanced toluene adsorption
## 1 Introduction
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), comprising transition metal ions (Zn, Co) and bridging imidazolate units, are an emerging class of porous materials with zeolite-type tetrahedral topologies [1]. As a subclass of metal organic frameworks (MOFs), ZIFs possess both high specific surface area and ultrahigh porosity and have excellent chemical and thermal stability [2, 3]. Owing to these fascinating physicochemical characteristics, ZIFs can be applied for various applications such as gas storage [4], adsorption/separation [5], sensors [6], biomedicine [7], supercapacitors [8, 9], and catalysis [10, 11]. To date, a large number of ZIFs, such as ZIF-8, ZIF-67, and ZIF-90, have been synthesized; among these, ZIF-90 is recognized as an attractive material for preparing gas separation membranes owing to its special pore structure [12]. However, ZIF-90 particles obtained using conventional solvothermal methods are considerably large; thus, they are inappropriate to be applied in thin mixed-matrix membranes, which generally require particles of submicronscale or even smaller [13]. To date, many methods have been developed for decreasing the particle size of ZIF-90. For example, Nair et al. reported forming uniform ZIF-90 having a crystal size of $\sim850~\mathrm{nm}$ using nonsolventinduced crystallization methods [14]. Subsequently, Brown et al. [15] successfully decreased the particle size of ZIF-90 from 850 to $400~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . However, these methods usually require harsh conditions (such as high temperature) for its synthesis owing to the stronger acidity of $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ -hydrogen in the aldehyde group, which reduces the reactivity for ZIF-90 construction, thereby hindering the automatic formation of ZIF-90 at room temperature.
In addition, the presence of micropores and small mesopores $(<5~\mathrm{{nm})}$ in conventional ZIF-90 limits the diffusion of large molecules within and out of pore channels, which reduces the efficiency of diffusion and mass transfer [16, 17], particularly for larger biomolecules [18]. Therefore, extending the porosity of ZIF-90 into large mesopores $(>10~\mathrm{nm})$ or even macropores $(>50~\mathrm{nm})$ is necessary. To date, several techniques of increasing the pore size of ZIFs material have been proposed, such as amino acid-assisted methods [19], template [20], and composite materials [21, 22]. However, these methods are offset by unfavorable reaction conditions such as high temperature and long reaction time [23–26]. Therefore, an economical facile synthetic method that can decrease the crystal size and extend the pore size simultaneously is highly desirable.
In this study, we report a simple and facile method for rapidly synthesizing hierarchically porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals (particle sizes of $\sim300\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) at room temperature by introducing zinc oxide $(Z\mathrm{nO})$ . During the synthesis, $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ was used to form $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}},Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ hydroxy double salt (HDS), which could accelerate the nucleation rate of crystals and result in the rapid growth of ZIF-90. Therefore, the reaction time was drastically reduced to $5~\mathrm{min}$ . Furthermore, the morphology and particle size was directly tuned by changing the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , and the resulting hierarchically porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals showed high thermal stability. Moreover, the as-synthesized hierarchically porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals showed excellent performance for enhancing adsorption of gaseous toluene.
