## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Room-temperature rapid synthesis of nanoscale hierarchical porous ZIF-90
In the typical synthesis, firstly, 5 mmol $Z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ powder and 5 of zinc acetate dihydrate $\mathrm{(Zn(CH_{3}C O_{2})_{2}{\cdot}2H_{2}O)}$ were added to deionized water $(10~\mathrm{ml})$ and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $5~\mathrm{ml}$ ) and then continuous stirred for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. After that, the $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}},Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}})$ HDS was formed. Next, 3 mmol ligand (imidazole-2-carboxyaldehyde, ICA) dissolved in $8~\mathrm{ml}$ DMF, and then 2 ml (Zn, Zn) HDS was added to ligand solution under fast stirring at room
Figure 1 shows XRD patterns of as-synthesized ZIF-90_X $(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{A}$ , B, C, D) and the simulated ZIF-90 pattern from the Materials Studio package 7.0. All diffraction peak positions of ZIF-90_X $\mathbf{X}=\mathbf{A}$ , B, C) agree with those of the simulation data, indicating that the resulting ZIF-90_X samples had a framework connection similar to that of ZIF90 [15]. This result confirms that ZIF-90 crystals were successfully synthesized within 5 min at room temperature. However, broader XRD peaks were observed in the patterns of ZIF-90_X compared to the simulation result, which can be attributed to the abundance of lattice defects or the small crystallite size in ZIF-90_X samples [4]. In addition, the peak intensities of ZIF-90_A, ZIF-90_B, ZIF-90_C, and ZIF-90_D were obviously decreasing, which can be attributed to few or excess imidazole-2-carboxyaldehyde (ICA) that are unbeneficial to the crystallization of ZIF-90, while $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}=2.25$ is the optimal ratio to form ZIF crystals [27, 28]. Furthermore, when the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ was below 1.5 (for ZIF-90_D), some additional diffraction peaks were observed in the XRD patterns, indicating the formation of unknown crystals. This observation is attributed to the inability of a small number of ligands to terminate the crystal growth and stabilize positively charged nanocrystals in solution [29]. These results indicate that the ligand ICA is a key factor to modulate charge in solution for the formation of high-quality ZIF-90 crystals at room temperature.
SEM and TEM were used to track the morphology, particle size, and porosity of the as-synthesized ZIF-90_X 1 $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{A}}$ , B, C). As illustrated in Fig. 2a, SEM image shows that the ZIF-90_A comprised well-ordered rhombic dodecahedral nanoparticles with a diameter of $\sim300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The morphology was similar to the conventional ZIF-90 synthesized using a hydrothermal method [13], whereas the particle size of ZIF-90_A was considerably lesser than that of conventional ZIF-90 $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\r~}}\sim180~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ [16]. The small particle size in ZIF-90_A can be attributed to the fast nucleation $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~min}})$ [30]. In particular, ZIFs with dimensions of a nanometer scale leads to new possibilities in multiple fields such as separation and catalysis, while the applications of the micrometer level are considerably restricted [15, 31, 32]. Moreover, a number of voids encompassed by the walls composed of nanoparticles confirm the existence of mesopores and macropores [33]. In addition, when the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ increases from 3.0 to 3.75, the shape of ZIF-90 nanocrystals gradually becomes irregular with bumpy surfaces, as shown in Fig. 2b, c. These analyses indicate that molar ratios of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ have considerable influence on the morphology of ZIF-90 crystals, which can be attributed to the amounts of ligand ICA that can accelerate nucleation at different rates, consequently resulting in the occurrence of different morphologies [28, 34].
Note that additional visual evidence considering the particle size and porosity of ZIF-90_X can be acquired from TEM images. As shown in Fig. 2d, e, both ZIF-90_A and ZIF-90_B have small crystal sizes with a diameter of less than $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which are consistent with SEM results (Fig. 2a, b). The TEM image in Fig. 3f shows that ZIF$90\_\mathrm{C}$ comprised block-shaped nanoparticles that are different from ZIF-90_A and ZIF-90_B samples, indicating that the amount of ligand ICA is the major cause of variations in the crystal morphologies of these ZIF-90_X samples [28]. Furthermore, apparent pores can be observed
