$\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distributions (PSDs, calculated from nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT) analysis) were used to investigate the porosity of ZIF-90_X samples. As shown in Fig. 3a, ZIF-90_X and C-ZIF-90 samples exhibited a hybrid type I/IV behavior, featured by a sharp increase in ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake at relative pressure of $P/P_{0}<0.02\$ (Type I) and an apparent hysteresis loop in the relative pressure range between 0.3 and 0.5 (Type IV). This behavior is related to the intrinsic micropores and mesopores in ZIF-90; however, all ZIF$90\underline{{\mathrm{~X~}}}$ showed an additional hysteresis loop at relative pressure of $P/P_{0}>0.8$ , indicating the introduction of extended mesopores and macropores [35]. Note that additional detailed information considering the porosity of ZIF$90\_X$ is acquired from the PSDs. As shown in Fig. 3b, other than the intrinsic micropores and mesopores $(<5~\mathrm{{nm})}$ , ZIF-90_X contains a lot of pores broadly distributed from 10 to over $200~\mathrm{nm}$ , which is absent from the C-ZIF-90. These results indicate the successful synthesis of hierarchical porous ZIF-90 with micropores, mesopores, and macropores.
The textural properties of hierarchical porous ZIF-90_X and C-ZIF-90 are summarized in Table 1. All ZIF-90_X samples exhibited higher BET surface areas $(S_{\mathrm{BET}})$ and Langmuir surface areas $(S_{\mathrm{Langmuir}})$ than C-ZIF-90, which can be attributed to the decrease in the crystal size can increase surface areas [36]. Furthermore, all ZIF-90_X showed higher total pore volumes $(V_{\mathrm{total}})$ and mesopore volumes $(V_{\mathrm{meso}})$ than C-ZIF-90, confirming the generation of mesopores and macropores in ZIF-90_X samples. In addition, these ZIF-90_X samples exhibited different $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ SLangmuir, $V_{\mathrm{total}},$ and $V_{\mathrm{meso}}$ , indicating that the porosity properties of hierarchical porous ZIF-90_X could be easily tuned by varying the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ .
To evaluate the thermal stability of the as-synthesized hierarchical porous ZIF-90_X, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed. As shown in Fig. 4, three main stages of weight loss can be observed; the initial step of the weight loss in the temperature range between 35 and $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can be attributed to the evaporation of ethanol and water [37]. The second step of the weight loss in the temperature range of $70{-}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can probably be ascribed to the removal of guest molecules trapped within the pore channels or framework [38]. The main loss occurred after $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ because of the structural decomposition of ZIF90_X crystals [39]. According to previous reports [16], the collapsed temperature of ZIF-90 synthesized by a conventional method is $\sim250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is lower than the temperature of the as-synthesized hierarchical porous ZIF$90\_X$ . This may be because of the decreased particle size of ZIF crystals [40]. Hierarchical porous ZIF-90_X showed a consistent trend of weight loss, implying that the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ had no significant influence on their thermal stability. These results indicate that the as-synthesized hierarchically porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals have excellent thermal stability.
According to previous studies [41, 42], (Zn, Zn) HDS has an excellent anion exchange rate and can accelerate the rapid formation of MOFs. Therefore, a possible mechanism for the room-temperature rapid preparation of hierarchical porous ZIF-90 by adding $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ is illustrated in Scheme 1. In the initial stage, the added $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ reacts with $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}C O_{2})_{2}\cdot2H_{2}O}$ to form (Zn, Zn) HDS intermediates, which exhibits an excellent anion exchange ability in the ligand solution. Then, when the HDS was added to ligand solution, because of the high rate of anion exchange of HDS, ligand ICA rapidly combines with (Zn, Zn) HDS to from ZIF-90 crystals, in which some $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ dispersed in solution was enscapsulated into the crystal. Subsequently, when the $Z\mathrm{nO}$ was removed by a simple washing and drying process (Sect. 2.1 for detail), mesopores and macropores with microporous walls were formed. However, it is worth mentioning that because of the decrease in synthesis time, the resulting products exhibited smaller crystal size, and similar conclusions were drawn in the earlier works by Terfort et al. [43] and Vajo et al. [30]
Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) trigger severe environmental issues such as ozone depletion, photochemical smog, and global warming [44, 45]. Toluene, a kind of volatile organic compound, renders them pivotal targets for control, even at very light concentration [46]. ZIFs exhibit excellent adsorption performance in capturing
VOCs owing to their large surface area, superior thermal stability, and chemical stability [47]. Here, the as-synthesized hierarchical porous ZIF-90 was used as an adsorbent to capture toluene. Figure 5a shows the gaseous toluene adsorption isotherms over ZIF-90_X ( $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{A}}$ , B, C) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ . At a low relative pressure of $P/P_{0}<0.1$ , three isotherms exhibited a steep rise in the uptake, which is related to the monolayer adsorption of toluene in micropores [48, 49]. The isotherm increases with the increase in relative pressures; however, the rate of increase is slow and depends on the amount of toluene absorbed. Comparatively, the loading of toluene on ZIF-90_A is always higher than that on ZIF-90_B because of the higher surface area and pore volume of ZIF-90_A [20, 47]. Nevertheless, the saturation adsorption capacity of toluene on ZIF-90_A and ZIF-90_B is $340\ \mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, the saturation adsorption capacity of toluene on ZIF-90_C is $317~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , which was smaller than ZIF-90_A and ZIF-90_B $(340\ \mathrm{mg{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ . The low adsorption capacity of ZIF-90_C can be attributed to low degree of crystallinity. Moreover, considering the excellent thermal stability of hierarchical porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals, the as-synthesized hierarchical porous ZIF-90_X ( $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{A}}$ , B, C) samples can have good repeatability for VOCs adsorption. Figure 5b shows the saturation capacities of toluene onto various adsorbents. The as-synthesized hierarchical porous ZIF-90 samples exhibit significantly higher toluene saturation uptake than conventional microporous adsorbents such as ZIF-67 and UiO- $66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , which can be attributed to the introduction of large pores into the microporous ZIFs [38].
## 4 Conclusion
In summary, hierarchical porous ZIF-90 nanocrystals with micropores, mesopores, and macropores were synthesized within $5~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, via the introduction of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to form HDS, which is found to increase ZIF crystallization. Owing to the reduced synthesis time achieved using this process, the crystal sizes of ZIF-90 were $\sim$ $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is less than that of the conventional ZIF90. Compared with the conventional ZIF-90, the as-synthesized hierarchical porous ZIF-90 exhibited excellent thermal stability because of the decreased crystal sizes. The morphology and particle size of hierarchical porous ZIF-90 could be easily controlled by the molar ratio of $\mathrm{ICA}/\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ . The as-synthesized ZIF-90 has high toluene vapor uptake of $340\ \mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ and combines the advantages of micropores, mesopores, and macropores. The reduced crystal sizes, extended pore size, and good thermal stability provides the possibility for practical application in the use of ZIF-90.
